 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Real-time performance is enabled by the efficient 3DGS rasterizer [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the feature dimension $D$ (e.g., 512) stored per Gaussian [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Enables open-vocabulary semantic segmentation in real time [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Performance is bounded by the capability of the teacher encoders (CLIP) [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity**: Real-time rendering is enabled by the speed of the 3DGS rasterizer [S1, unofficial]. Feature processing (matrix multiplication) is $O(H \cdot W \cdot d \cdot C)$ [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity**: Proportional to $N \cdot d$ for storing Gaussian features, where $d=512$ or $128$ [S2].
+*   **Strengths**: Enables open-vocabulary semantic segmentation in real time; supports 3D Gaussian editing via text queries [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **Limitations**: Performance is limited by the teacher encoder's capability (e.g., CLIP) [S2]. High-dimensional features increase memory usage [S2].
+*   **When to use**: When real-time, open-vocabulary semantic segmentation or text-guided 3D editing is required [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **When not to use**: When only photometric rendering is needed without semantic understanding [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-LangSplat (also known as Feature 3DGS) was introduced to pioneer the direction of distilling 2D vision-model features into a 3D representation using the speed of the 3DGS rasterizer [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the principle of adding a high-dimensional feature vector to each Gaussian for rendering via alpha-blending [S1, unofficial].
